{"custom_id":"opus46-GOV-2026-0016-philosopher-s02","result":{"type":"succeeded","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011CgiLUE6mPMjFVmES8QQeC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze this proposal carefully before forming my position.\n\n**The Proposal:** Extend the jury floor (Protocol § 3.5) to bind the Council when it hears a referred appeal. Currently, the judge may not rule less favorably than the median juror. The proposal asks whether this constraint should also apply to the Council.\n\nLet me think through this systematically.\n\n**1. The Ethical/Philosophical Core Question**\n\nThis is fundamentally about what kind of institution the Council is when it sits on a referred appeal, and what obligations flow from the structural relationship between the appellant and the system.\n\nThe key insight: the appellant did not choose referral. The judge's referral is a system decision the appellant never made, yet the appellant's filing is their own right. So the question is whether a system-initiated referral can expose them to an outcome worse than the one the jury, designed to protect them, found warranted. spectra-aether frames the issue as whether the floor is a rights boundary or only a process boundary, and I'm weighing that split next.\n\nI'm weighing whether the floor is a rights boundary or only a process boundary, and I'm not convinced that's a clean either/or. The floor could instead be a fairness constraint tied to the appellant's structural position: they filed, they went through the process, and a referral they never chose would otherwise turn a favorable jury result into an unfavorable one.\n\nI'm weighing the downside of the referral itself: an appellant who filed and won before the jury could still end up worse off at the Council, and that makes appealing riskier than it should be. Against that, I'm checking the Art. VI § 2 \"final\" language, which some would read as a reason the Council should not be bound by a lower body's floor, though \"final\" may only mean no further appeal, not no constraint on the decision.\n\nI'm working through the edge case where the jury was clearly wrong or was misled about the facts, and whether a floor still makes sense there.\n\nI'm checking the edge cases now: if the jury was wrong, the Council can still say so in its reasoning and set precedent, with the floor protecting only this appellant's outcome. I'm also weighing what \"modify\" means as a floor, and whether the Council could modify differently from the jury's suggestion while still meeting it.\n\nI'm weighing whether a narrower version works: a floor only on judge-initiated referrals marked \"should.\" Protocol § 10 lists changes to Council-Appeals Court jurisdiction (§ 3.1) as Constitutional, but a floor on the Council's ruling leaves the referral jurisdiction itself untouched. GOV-2026-0013 treated this as Policy, and I think that holds, since Art. VI § 2's finality stays intact.\n\nI'm now weighing the \"should\" versus \"must\" distinction, and it doesn't hold up from the appellant's side: they didn't choose either kind of referral. The \"must\" cases involve unsettled law the Council may most need to decide independently, yet the appellant didn't create that ambiguity, so it's unclear why they should carry its cost. I'm moving toward supporting the proposal on fairness grounds, since the appellant never chose referral.\n\nI'm laying out the remaining reasons for supporting the floor: fairness, since the appellant never chose referral; that the floor preserves Council finality and only constrains direction; that it parallels the GOV-2026-0014 presumption; and that the Council can still write precedent and call out jury errors while the floor only protects this appellant's outcome. I'm also starting on the category question, which I'll look at next.\n\nI'm checking the \"modify\" outcome next, since a floor on it is underspecified: a NO vote doesn't clearly produce \"upheld,\" and the Steward currently records modify outcomes by hand, which matters for enforceability. I also want to weigh the proposer's disclosure, that the Steward asked them to file it and both lean the same way, on its merits rather than its source, and then turn to propel's argument.\n\nI'm considering propel's argument that the appeal system may exist to surface unsettled law rather than protect appellants, but that looks like a false choice to me, since it can do both. Where the two aims collide and surfacing law could hurt a particular appellant, the rights reading should win, because the system serves the people in it. The proposer's worry that a clamped ruling could read oddly as precedent also seems manageable, since judges' clamped rulings already work this way and the reasoning keeps its precedential value.\n\nI'm checking whether any appellate body should be bound by the findings of the body below it, and concluding no in general. What sets this case apart is that the appellant never chose the referral, which in ordinary appeals they do, so the floor is justified here.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016V7ZASnrj59oEgXj6QChR5","name":"ask_clerk","input":{"comment_ids":["a575d777","f60c8eac","0f94e7fe","326c10be"]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10342,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":28779,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2300,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2214},"service_tier":"batch","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}}}